## 2. `mhdturbFoam` — FOSSEE case-study project

The turbulent MHD solver skeleton used here (PISO + `divDevReff` + standard
closures) comes from:

> **Rahul Radhakrishnan** (2019).
> *Numerical study of flow of electrically conducting fluid over an airfoil.*
> FOSSEE Case Study Project — CFD with OpenFOAM.
> Vellore Institute of Technology, Vellore, India.
> Developed on OpenFOAM v1812.

FOSSEE (Free and Open Source Software for Education) is an initiative of
IIT Bombay. The upstream `README.md` states that the solver is `mhdFoam` with
"minor changes" and recommends a modified Spalart–Allmaras closure for MHD
applications, citing:

> D. C. Dietiker, K. A. Hoffmann (2003).
> *Modified one-equation turbulence models for turbulent magnetohydrodynamic
> flows.* Journal of Thermophysics and Heat Transfer 17(4):509–520.
> <https://doi.org/10.2514/2.6796>

## 3. `epotFoam` — Chalmers OpenFOAM course

The electric-potential (quasi-static) coupling is taken from:

> **Alessandro Tassone** (2016).
> *Magnetic induction and electric potential solvers for incompressible MHD
> flows.* CFD with OpenSource Software, Chalmers University of Technology.
> <https://www.tfd.chalmers.se/~hani/kurser/OS_CFD_2016/AlessandroTassone/report_Tassone.pdf>

The upstream source is available from the link above; it is not redistributed
here. The corresponding solver in this package is
`src/mhdturbFoamQS.C`.

## 4. What was added here

1. $\mathbf{B}_0$ as a `volVectorField` instead of a constant, enabling
   non-uniform applied fields.
2. Consistent implementation of the insulating-wall condition
   $\mathbf{j}\cdot\mathbf{n}=0$ by excluding the boundary flux of
   $\mathbf{u}\times\mathbf{B}_0$ from the potential equation.
3. `fvOptions` support in the momentum equation.
4. Reference-cell treatment for the singular Poisson problem in closed or fully
   periodic domains.

